Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 09/19/2024]
1.  Er = 301 - 2677 keV: measured strengths from Johnson et al. 1974, Endt and van der Leun 1978 and Iliadis et al. 1994, where the strengths have been normalized 
    to our adopted standard value for Elab = 860 keV. 
2.  Er = 735 - 2677 keV [except standard resonance at Er = 837 keV]: no individual uncertainties on the strengths are listed in Johnson et al. 1974 or Endt and van 
    der Leun 1978; Johnson et al. state an overall value of 30%, which we adopt.
3.  Er = 51, 167 keV: Gp from (3He,d) experiment of Iliadis et al. 1994; partial width ratios from Ross et al. 1995; Gg = 0.5 eV is an inconsequential guess; 
    level lifetimes are not known.
4.  Er = 88, 234 keV: levels reported in Roepke et al. 2002, supposedly of negative parity and high spin, i.e., 4- or 5-. We assumed 5- for an upper limit estimation.
    Gg = 0.5 eV is a guess.
5.  Er 342 keV: level reported in Iliadis et al. 1996; 2+ (ell = 0) assumed for upper limit estimation. Gp is estimated assuming C2S < 0.0067 using (3He,d) 
    experiment of Iliadis et al. 1994. Ga upper limit is found assuming C2S_a < 0.01 (level not seen in (6Li,d) spectrum of Iliadis et al. 1994). Gg = 0.5 eV is a 
    guess. 
6.  Er = 382 keV: Jp = (<= 5-) reported; ell_p = 0 + 2, 1, and 0 can be excluded based on wg_pg comparison to directly measured upper limit.
7.  Er = 417 keV: Gp from (3He,d) information given in Iliadis et al. 1994. We assume that 8919 keV level (Roepke et al. 2002) is the same as the 8923 keV state 
    (Iliadis et al. 1994); spin/parity can then be restricted to Jp=(4,5)-. Stripping data are best described by l=3, although a small l=1 component cannot be 
    excluded; the l=1 component from fit yields a (p,g) resonance strength in excess of experimental upper limit; thus we assume pure l=3 transfer.
8.  Direct capture is calculated using C2S from (3He,d) works of Moinester & Parker Alford 1970 and Iliadis et al. 1994.

